  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 240 AM CST Sat Nov 28 2026\n\nThe weak trough which moved into the Upper Midwest today has \ntaken on the form of a warm front associated with a low pressure \ncenter over southern Manitoba. With the warm front pushing into \nWI early this morning, this has nudged most of the cloud cover \noutside of the WFO MPX coverage area, save for some lingering \ncloud cover over western WI. This also has allowed a few spots \nof FZFG to develop over central MN, but these are expected to \ndissipate come sunrise with only passing high cirrus through the\nmorning hours. With the coverage area being effectively warm- \nsectored today, we shall have high temperatures cruise to the \nmid 40s to around 50 degrees today, obviously well above normal \nfor late November. However, with the warm front will be its \nfollowing cold front, and this will swing southeast across the \narea this evening through tonight. Though no precipitation will \ncome from this frontal passage, an increase in low clouds is \nexpected late this afternoon through tonight. The clouds will \nact as a bit of a blanket to prevent truly effective CAA from \nimpacting the area, but lows tonight into Sunday morning will \nstill drop below freezing, ranging in the mid-to-upper 20s. The \ncolder air will most certainly be felt Sunday through Monday as \nhighs remain in the 30s both days with lows in the teens Sunday \nnight into Monday morning. \n\nHowever, these values won't persist as southerly flow returns\nMonday night and more in earnest on Tuesday, bringing \ntemperatures back to above normal levels for the start of \nDecember. Highs will remain in the 40s from Tuesday through the \nrest of next week, peaking on Wednesday when highs near and \nsouth of the Minnesota River may hit the lower 50s. There are \nsome indications of a frontal passage near the end of next week \nwhich could bring precipitation chances to most of our coverage \narea. However, the system does not look impactful and looks to \nhave rain as the prevailing precipitation type with some snow \nshowers mixed in for western and central MN into western WI. \nThus, we are still on track to not only have a November without \nany snow at any of our 4 climate sites (MSP, STC, EAU and our \noffice in Chanhassen) but also to hit, if not extend, the latest\ndate of having measurable snow at MSP, which is currently \nDecember 3 1928.",
